phantom-mirage for Debian
-------------------------

I've added some of the contributed scripts at 
http://www.citi.umich.edu/u/provos/honeyd/contrib.html

However, some of the most complex ones (POP.emulator and issemul8) should
probably be packaged separately [As a matter of fact issemul8 is packaged
as 'iisemulator']

Quick start: phantom-mirage-setup
---------------------------------

  sudo phantom-mirage-setup            # or: --interface IF --network CIDR

It uses the default-route interface (or --interface), picks a /29 near
the top of that interface's subnet whose addresses do not answer
ping/arping (or --network), writes a clean /etc/default/phantom-mirage
(the old one is kept as /etc/default/phantom-mirage.bak-<time>), sets
RUN="yes", checks the file, runs "systemctl enable" + "restart", and prints
the service status and a one-line test hint. --dry-run only shows what it
would write and do.

From a release folder (the .debs, SHA256SUMS, install.sh,
phantom-mirage-uninstall), one command installs and configures:

  sudo sh install.sh

It checks SHA256SUMS, picks the .debs for this distro (ubuntu26.04 or
debian13) and architecture, purges any installed Phantom Mirage first
(config included; meant for test boxes), installs and runs
phantom-mirage-setup (extra arguments are passed on to it).

To remove everything again (service, packages, /etc/default/phantom-mirage,
/etc/phantom-mirage, /var/lib/phantom-mirage, /var/log/phantom-mirage, the
phantom-mirage user/group; promiscuous mode off on the interface):

  sudo phantom-mirage-uninstall        # --yes: no prompt, --dry-run: show only

Configuring by hand
-------------------

  1. Find the decoy interface:  ip -br a
  2. Edit /etc/default/phantom-mirage:
       INTERFACE="enp0s5"            # from step 1
       NETWORK="192.168.50.8/29"     # small, UNUSED block on that LAN
     One KEY=VALUE per line, values with spaces quoted as a whole, no
     quote spanning lines, comments only on their own lines. The file is
     NOT sourced by a shell or read with systemd's EnvironmentFile=; the
     service (/usr/lib/phantom-mirage/pm-start) refuses anything else
     with the line number, e.g.
       phantom-mirage: /etc/default/phantom-mirage line 31: unbalanced or stray quote in NETWORK="10.211.55.200/29 (...)
     NETWORK wider than /24 is refused unless ALLOW_WIDE_NETWORK=yes.
     Never include addresses used by real hosts or the gateway.
  3. sudo systemctl enable --now phantom-mirage
  4. Check:  systemctl status phantom-mirage ; journalctl -u phantom-mirage

If the settings are missing or wrong, the service stops at once (exit
status 78, no restart loop) and the journal says why, e.g.
  phantom-mirage: INTERFACE is not set in /etc/default/phantom-mirage; ...
Fix the file and run "sudo systemctl restart phantom-mirage". The same
check can be run by hand:
  sudo /usr/lib/phantom-mirage/check-default /etc/default/phantom-mirage

If you use farpd to answer ARP for the decoys, give it the same small
block (farpd is a separate package and is not started from this file).

Notice also that in order for phantom-mirage to work you will probably need
a user-space ARP daemon that answers requests for IP addresses
which have been configured in phantom-mirage. Such a daemon is provided in
the 'farpd' package. You can also make phantom-mirage work if it's setup
as the default router on your environment for the networks you want 
to simulate. The main point being that if you do not configure your
environment or your phantom-mirage host to have traffic directed to the networks
you want to setup go through your phantom-mirage host then your phantom-mirage host
will not get hit with any incoming traffic to those networks.

Personality templates are provided under /usr/share/phantom-mirage, and a sample
configuration file under /usr/share/doc/phantom-mirage/examples.

Phantom Mirage is provided with an init.d script but it is not active when 
installed. 

Restrictions
------------

Installing phantom-mirage in a restricted environment is recommended.
This should be done in order to prevent security issues specially when using
the scripts provided. Notice that the scripts are low-interaction but
they have _not_ been audited for security vulnerabilities.

Some steps would include:

1- created a jail for it (using chroot, user mode linux or your preferred 
software of choice)
2- run phantom-mirage as a non-root user. The default configuration for phantom-mirage
in Debian uses the 'phantom-mirage' user.
3- run the scripts as a user with very low privileges. Notice that 
you can only do this if phantom-mirage runs as root or the user running it has the
CAP_SETUID capability. If running as root this is done by modifying the
default template provided with phantom-mirage:
----------------------------------------------------------------------
set template uid 65534 gid 65534
----------------------------------------------------------------------

If you do run it as a low user you will have to grant it access to the
log files in order for phantom-mirage to properly work. Otherwise, no logfile
will get written and phantom-mirage might abort when trying to do so. This is
usually setup automatically but you might want to review the permissions
of /var/log/phantom-mirage

Notice that some scripts will write to a log file (in some cases it
is hardcoded in the script, in other cases it needs to be defined in the
LOG environment variable). Make sure you review what a script does before 
using it in a honeypot environment and that it will have enough 
privileges to work properly.

Feedback, however, in order to improve this package is encouraged. 
Please mail ddejager@dandejager.com, or submit bugs using 'reportbug'

Running in a chroot
-------------------

As described before, it is recommended that you run Phantom Mirage in a
restricted environment. If you plan to run Phantom Mirage in a chroot environment
you can use the 'makejail' software or just setup a minimal Debian
system with 'debootstrap' and install Phantom Mirage in it.

For more information read the Debian Securing Manual, more specifically
"General chroot and suid paranoia", available at 
http://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/securing-debian-howto/ch-sec-services.en.html#s-chroot
and "Making chrooted environments automatically", available at
http://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/securing-debian-howto/ch-sec-services.en.html#s-auto-chroot

